---
title: Entropy and the Art of Gardening
date: 2025-11-20
coverImage: /images/journal/entropy-and-the-art-of-gardening/coverImage.png
description: >-
  Software, like a garden, tends towards chaos. The second law of thermodynamics
  applies to codebases just as it does to the universe.
---
I used to think of software engineering as construction. You build the foundation, you frame the walls, you put on the roof, and you're done. The building stands.

I was wrong. Software engineering is gardening.

## The Weed of Technical Debt

You don't "finish" a garden. You tend to it. You water it. And most importantly, you weed it.

Entropy is the weed of the digital world. It creeps in quietly. A quick fix here, a duplicated function there, a dependency that hasn't been updated in two years. Left unchecked, entropy consumes structure. It turns a clean, elegant architecture into a tangled mess of vines and thorns.

> "The programmer has to be possessed of the ability to compose programs of great complexity and yet keeps them simple."

## Pruning as Progress

In gardening, pruning is painful. You cut away healthy branches to allow the plant to thrive. In coding, we call this refactoring. We delete code—sometimes code we loved writing—because it no longer serves the ecosystem.

It feels counterintuitive to delete work. But just as a rose bush produces better blooms when it's not wasting energy on dead wood, a codebase becomes more powerful when it is lean.

## Embracing the Seasons

Gardens have seasons. They have times of explosive growth and times of dormancy. Projects are the same. There are sprints of intense feature development, and there should be winters of maintenance, of documentation, of rest.

If we try to force a garden to bloom in perpetual summer, we exhaust the soil. If we try to force a team to ship in perpetual crunch mode, we exhaust the soul.

To accept entropy is not to give up. It is to understand the nature of the work. We are not builders of stone monuments. We are caretakers of living, breathing systems. And the most beautiful gardens are not the ones that are "finished," but the ones that are loved.
